IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Row il-60. NOUNS of a cross-reference and of a printed date pair. No rule is written here; the facts below are what a reader of the texts could state.
-- TARGET COMPILER: main. No REFUSE, no section GIVEN, no TYPICALLY, no YMD, no sets: a case the sources do not answer is NOTHING from a MAYBE.
--
-- This row is NOT an encoding of the Criminal Procedure Law [Consolidated Version], 5742-1982. It records ONE reference: the words of s 220ג(ג) that name paragraph (4) of s 220ד(א),
-- and the last day that paragraph's own words print. A second reference, inside the same sentence of s 220ג(ג), is recorded beside it as the sound control.
-- A reference whose target prints a last day that has passed is kept in an expected-red file; the control is not.
--
-- THE KINDS BELOW ARE MINE, not the Law's: each is a short plain description I wrote from reading the citing words and the target.
-- The words of the texts are quoted beside each value in the references module (`src:` lines are the Law's consolidation, `ext:[SH 2999 ...]` lines are typed from a page image of the official instrument).

§ `Nouns of a cross-reference`

-- Two kinds of reading, from the strongest to the weakest this row has.
-- The second kind is the honest name for every limb here: the official page was read, but that no LATER instrument touched the words rests on the consolidation's amendment tag alone.
DECLARE `Where a reading comes from` IS ONE OF
    `the official instrument, read from its page image`
    `the official instrument, read from its page image; that no later instrument touched the words is CONSOLIDATION-ONLY`

DECLARE `A cross-reference` HAS
    `the citing words are in` IS A STRING
    `the citing words name` IS A STRING
    `the target is labelled` IS A STRING
    `the target exists in the official text read` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the last day printed in the target` IS A MAYBE DATE
    `the first day certainly after the printed day` IS A MAYBE DATE
    `limb (a) was read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`
    `limb (b) was read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`

§ `Nouns of a printed date pair`

-- A Hebrew date and a Gregorian date printed together. The Hebrew date is written in Latin letters here, so that this file holds no Hebrew that its source does not hold; the Hebrew is on the `ext:` lines.
-- The third field is what the Hebrew-calendar routine in tools/hebcal.py gives for the Hebrew date; the routine is not part of this module.
DECLARE `A printed date pair` HAS
    `the Hebrew date, transliterated` IS A STRING
    `the Gregorian date printed with it` IS A DATE
    `the Gregorian date the calendar routine gives` IS A DATE
    `the pair was read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`
